<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lorsch</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lorsch is a town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between Worms and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odenwald">Odenwald</a>. It grew around Lorsch Abbey, founded in 764, which became one of the richest and most famous monasteries of the Carolingian empire and the burial place of East Frankish kings. The abbey's gatehouse, the Königshalle, is one of the best-preserved Carolingian buildings and part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lorsch.jpg" alt="Lorsch" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kuebi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lorsch_Rathaus_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lorsch is a town in the Bergstraße district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, about 60 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>. It lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> rift about 5 km west of the Bergstraße and just west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odenwald">Odenwald</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> to the south, not far from the lower reaches of the Weschnitz. Its neighbours include Einhausen, Bensheim, Heppenheim, Lampertheim and Bürstadt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The abbey was founded in 764 by the Frankish Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cancor">Cancor</a> and his mother Williswinda, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedictines">Benedictines</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorze-abbey">Gorze Abbey</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> settled it. It was one of the major centres of Carolingian art, and several Carolingian kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> were buried there. A document of 885 calls it Lauressam, the source of the town's present name. In the Early and High Middle Ages it was a powerful imperial monastery, with holdings in the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odenwald">Odenwald</a>, on the Bergstraße, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-hesse">Rhenish Hesse</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><p>In the civil war arising from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a> in the 11th century the abbey lost much of its holdings to the nobility. In the late 12th century an attempt was made to reorganise its administration with a record of its old deeds, the Lorsch codex. Even so, in 1232 Lorsch was awarded to the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and newly settled by Premonstratensians. Mainz and the Electorate of the Palatinate then disputed who should hold the vogt rights. Of the Carolingian abbey, parts of which have been unearthed, the gatehall from about 800 has been preserved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lorsch Abbey has been named a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>, and the Königshalle, declared a site in 1991, appears in the town's coat of arms along with the red cross formerly borne by the abbey. The town has a museum centre with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of monastic history, folklore and tobacco, and the open-air museum Lauresham. The town hall was built between 1714 and 1715. Lorsch lies on the Nibelungenbahn railway between Bensheim and Worms, and the Autobahn A 67 runs through its territory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lorsch” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The counts of Katzenelnbogen first appear around 1095 as advocates of the Bishopric of Worms and the abbey of <b>Lorsch</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-katzenelnbogen">County of Katzenelnbogen</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lorsch</b> was founded in 764 by the Frankish count Cancor and his mother Williswinda, and handed to Archbishop Chrodegang of Metz.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-lorsch">Imperial Abbey of Lorsch</a></li></ul></section>
